---
import Guide from '../../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import swe from '../../../data/swe.json';
import { HOOKS, WHEN } from '../../../data/swe-prose';
import { url } from '../../../lib/url';

// Base's floor hook first: it runs under every rulebook. Then swe's, by tier.
const ORDER = { locked: 0, recommended: 1, optional: 2 } as const;
const all = [
  ...swe.base.hooks.map((h) => ({ ...h, from: 'base' })),
  ...swe.hooks.map((h) => ({ ...h, from: 'swe' })),
].sort((a, b) => (a.from === b.from ? ORDER[a.tier as keyof typeof ORDER] - ORDER[b.tier as keyof typeof ORDER] : a.from === 'base' ? -1 : 1));

const count = (tier: string) => all.filter((h) => h.tier === tier).length;
---

<Guide
  title="Hooks"
  description="Every hook in kerby's swe rulebook: when it runs, what it blocks or warns about, what it misses, and how to turn it off.">
  <section aria-labelledby="top">
    <h1 id="top">Hooks</h1>
    <p class="lead">The few rules that a script checks, not just the agent.</p>
    <p>
      A hook is a small script your agent tool runs at a fixed moment, such as before a shell command.
      A hook reads the command, not the conversation. That makes it hard to skip, and also limits what it
      can see. swe ships {swe.hooks.length} hooks, and the base floor adds {swe.base.hooks.length} more.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="install">
    <h2 id="install">How install offers them</h2>
    <p>
      kerby install shows every hook in a table, then asks: all, choose, or none. Each hook has a tier.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Tier</th><th scope="col">Hooks</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>locked</td><td>{count('locked')}</td><td>Comes with any hook install. You cannot skip it on its own — only by choosing none.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>recommended</td><td>{count('recommended')}</td><td>Blocks an action. You can skip it, and kerby status keeps telling you it is not enforcing.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>optional</td><td>{count('optional')}</td><td>Only warns. You can skip it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A hook works only after install and only where your tool runs it. Claude Code runs them. Codex
      does not. Run <code>kerby status</code> to see which hooks are really enforcing, and
      {' '}<code>kerby hooks</code> to see what install would add, without changing anything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="list">
    <h2 id="list">Every hook</h2>
    {all.map((h) => {
      const p = HOOKS[h.script!];
      return (
        <article class="hook" aria-labelledby={`h-${h.script}`}>
          <h3 id={`h-${h.script}`}>
            {p.name}
            <span class="chip">{h.script}</span>
          </h3>
          <dl>
            <dt>Runs</dt>
            <dd>{WHEN[h.matcher as string]}{h.from === 'base' && ' — from the base floor, under every rulebook'}</dd>
            <dt>Result</dt>
            <dd>{h.blocks ? 'Blocks the action' : 'Warns only, never blocks'} · tier {h.tier}</dd>
            <dt>Checks</dt>
            <dd>{h.checks.map((c) => <code>{c}</code>).reduce((acc: unknown[], c, i) => (i ? [...acc, ' ', c] : [c]), [])}</dd>
            <dt>What it does</dt>
            <dd>{p.does}</dd>
            <dt>What it misses</dt>
            <dd>{p.gaps}</dd>
            <dt>Turn it off</dt>
            <dd>{p.off}</dd>
          </dl>
        </article>
      );
    })}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="git">
    <h2 id="git">The git pre-commit hook</h2>
    <p>
      The secret scan above runs before the agent’s commit command, so it reads the command text. It
      cannot see <code>git add x && git commit</code> in one command, an alias, or a path in a variable.
    </p>
    <p>
      kerby install also offers the same scan as a git pre-commit hook. git hands that hook the real
      staged files, so those cases are covered. It lives in your clone only — git never shares hooks with
      teammates — and <code>git commit --no-verify</code> skips it for one commit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="blind">
    <h2 id="blind">What no hook can see</h2>
    <p>Hooks run at the tool call. Some rules live in the conversation, where no hook can look:</p>
    <ul class="blind">
      <li>a secret printed into the chat;</li>
      <li>instructions hidden in a file the agent reads;</li>
      <li>a test run that reaches a production system;</li>
      <li>whether the agent really ran the quality gate before it said “done”.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These rules are instructions only. When the hooks are not installed, every rule is. The
      <a href={url('rulebooks/swe/#map')}>map on the overview</a> shows the difference.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>

<style>
  .hook {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .hook h3 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.35rem 0.75rem;
  }

  .hook dl {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 9rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.6rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .hook dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--structure);
    padding-top: 0.2rem;
  }

  .hook dd {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 60ch;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .hook dl {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 0.15rem;
    }

    .hook dd {
      margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    }
  }

  .blind {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.35rem;
  }
</style>
